  "strategy": "Author: Keisuke Nishimura\nTitle: Multi-resolution Poisson Optimization\nRationale: Poisson's hedgehog theorem ensures that any continuous function with zero mean in R^2 can be represented exactly by its Bessel basis. This allows us to represent any function on the circle that integrates to zero with just a single parameter. We extend this one-dimensional analog for the circle and use it to represent step functions with jump discontinuities. This multi-scale representation yields a convex optimization problem that can be solved with a conic quadratic solver.\n\n1. Approach and rationale:\nWe represent the step function as a Poisson kernel composed of a few parameters. This reduces the dimensionality while preserving the integral constraint and allowing sharp transitions. The objective function is re-formulated as a logarithmic barrier in each basis vector component. The optimization becomes convex in the transformed space. We use a multi-resolution strategy: start with a coarse grid with few Poisson modes, then refind and optimize at finer grids with more modes.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nWe represent h(x) on [0,2] with Poisson basis scaling functions. The basis functions are:\n    \u03c6_k(x) = J_{0}(k*x) / (k)^0.5   (with appropriate domain mapping)\n\nThe function h(x) can be written as:\n    h(x) = (1/\u221a\u03c0) * \u2211_{k} c_k * \u03c6_k(x)\n\nConstraints:\n\u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1, and 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n\nObjective:\nmin C = max_{k} \u222b_0^{2-k} h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nWe approximate C by:\n    \u2211_{k} [\u222b_0^{2-k} h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx] + \u03bb\u00b7||c||\u2081\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n    a. Construct a parent grid at specified resolution with Poisson coefficients\n    b. Project the initial_h_values into the Poisson basis\n    c. Slice cost function as sum( log(h(x)+1) + log(1-h(x)+1) )\n    d. Solve the optimization problem with CVXOPT/Eigen\n    e. Use auto-differentiation through chain rule for gradient computation\n    f. Implement multi-resolution phase: \n       - Phase1: Coarse grid (n=280) optimization\n       - Phase2: Grid refinement to multiples of base for improved detail\n    g. Export solution by converting Poisson coefficients back to grid values\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n    - Grid sizes: [280, 560, 960, 1120]\n    - Basis: Poisson scaling bases with 16-64 modes depending on grid size\n    - Temperature control for optimization: \n        t0 = 300, k=0.99, t_final=15\n    - Optimization iterations: 300 (out of 500) per temperature stage\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n    - Dominant cost is matrix inversion at each gradient step: O(m*k^2)\n    - Grid doubling increases costs 4x due to additional modes\n    - Fallback: Return best solution at any grid up to time expiration\n    - Integral-preservation: Auto-normalize after resampling\n\n6. Validation contract:\n    - Check h(i) in [0,1] for all i\n    - Verify integral = n/2\n    - Recompute C5 via numerical integration with full trapezoidal rule\n    - Compare with original code's correlate function\n\nImplementation details:\n    - Use iterative collocation of matrices for Poisson operator\n    - Store basis functions as tuple of:\n          (array_weights, array_shifts)\n    - Compute gradient term-heading if could not see exactly past entire needed context may seem moot our plan is distantly different than the sample or previous proposals.\n\nThe advantages of this approach:\n    - Reduced dimensionality allows global optimization\n    - Convexity enables use of optimal solvers\n    - Dynamic grid upgrade preserves all data in basis space\n    - Lower run time due to dimension compression\n    - Memory efficiency: orders",
